From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 05:51:37 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] kboot: admit the J604 on the T8132 AOP handoff path

8333079a held the J604 back from T8132_PATH_AOP because its iop-aop-nub
aop-target (0) differs from the J713's (1). That property does not
reach the handoff: no m1n1, U-Boot or Linux code reads aop-target, and
the J713-qualified Linux AOP configuration writes the AOPt boot argument
0 whatever the ADT says.

What the AOP path reads matches. dt_set_t8132_aop() checks the AOP is
halted, programs the dart-aop bank selected by instance[1], reserves
iop-aop-nub segment-ranges and reserves the bank's merged windows. On
the J604:

- the 25G83 and 26B123 template ADTs carry the same dart-aop instance
  table, reg and dapf-instance-0 (16 slices) as the J713 ones;
- a 27.2 (26B123) runtime ADT of the J604 equals
  tests/data/j713-dapf-instance.json, the J713 runtime fixture the
  writer tests run on, in every field: the dart-aop, dart-mtp and
  dart-pmp instance tables, their reg windows after the arm-io ranges,
  dapf-instance-0, and the consumers' clock-gates;
- that runtime ADT has the five AOP segments (__TEXT, __DATA, __ETEXT,
  __EDATA, __OS_LOG), __ETEXT remapped to 0x10000000000 as on J713.

Every step still checks the live ADT and disables the AOP on any
failure, and the handoff only runs when the kernel DT asks for it in
/chosen linux-enablement-mac,dapf-handoff. The segments under the 25G83
stub iBoot, and that it leaves the AOP halted, are first-boot checks.

tests: test_t8132_aop_handoff_gate runs the production
dt_set_aop_firmware() and dt_set_t8132_aop() on libfdt trees for the
J604, J713 and an unqualified T8132 board, with the hardware steps
stubbed; it fails against the previous board table.
test_t8132_board_admission now expects the J604 on every path.

Hardware tested on: NOT YET
Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
---
